# 6. GENUINE MACHINE LEARNING — NO FABRICATED ACCURACY

Inspect the existing model-training, prediction and model-comparison implementations before modifying them.

Retain Logistic Regression, Random Forest, XGBoost or other existing models only where their implementations are valid, their dependencies are justified and their computational requirements are realistic.

Do not add a model merely because it is popular.

### Define the prediction target

Every model must have a documented target and forecast horizon.

For example, a model may estimate the direction of a stock over the next five trading sessions. Define exactly how the label is calculated, how observations are aligned and when a prediction is considered correct.

Do not mix a next-day target, five-day target and long-term investment outlook into one unexplained prediction.

### Data integrity

- Use chronological training, validation and test splits.
- Use walk-forward or other appropriate time-series validation.
- Prevent look-ahead bias and target leakage.
- Fit preprocessing and feature selection using training data only.
- Align news, prices, fundamentals and labels by their actual availability timestamps.
- Avoid using future financial statements or subsequently published information as if they were known at prediction time.
- Compare the model against a suitable simple baseline.
- Record model version, training period, target definition and feature set.

### Evaluation

Report real, reproducible metrics appropriate to the target, such as:

- Accuracy and balanced accuracy.
- Precision, recall and F1 where appropriate.
- Confusion matrix.
- ROC-AUC only when mathematically appropriate.
- Calibration metrics when probability estimates are displayed.
- Trading returns and drawdown only from a valid trading simulation.

Never insert hardcoded comparison scores, manually invented accuracy percentages or sample outputs into the production results.

If a model has not been trained or evaluated, show that status clearly.

If there is insufficient data to train or evaluate a model, explain the limitation.

### Predictions and uncertainty

- Show the forecast horizon.
- Explain whether the output is a classification, estimated return or calibrated probability.
- Display confidence or probability only when it has a valid mathematical interpretation.
- Avoid implying that a probability is a guarantee.
- Allow “Insufficient evidence” or “Model unavailable”.
- Do not force every stock into BUY, HOLD or SELL.

A simple technical rule may remain as a separately labelled rule-based signal, but it must never be represented as a trained ML model prediction.

